      So: one card per driver, truck and the carrier record, and `&s=` opens one.
      Same doctrine as the tiles, one level down — shareable, back-button walks
      the subjects he tried, and it bounds the page to a single subject instead of
      shipping 600 date inputs over one bar of signal.
  */}

  {/* The POST forms every card action submits. Rendered outside the date editor
      because a form may not contain another, and hidden because there is
      nothing in them to look at — a hidden form still submits. */}
  {
    view === 'unknown' && (
      <>
        <form method="POST" id={ACTION_FORMS.ask} class="hidden">
          <input type="hidden" name="intent" value="ask_driver_dates" />
        </form>
        <form method="POST" id={ACTION_FORMS.remind} class="hidden">
          <input type="hidden" name="intent" value="remind_me_later" />
        </form>
        <form method="POST" id={ACTION_FORMS.retire} class="hidden">
          <input type="hidden" name="intent" value="retire_driver" />
        </form>
        <form method="POST" id={ACTION_FORMS.askAll} class="hidden">
          <input type="hidden" name="intent" value="ask_all_drivers" />
        </form>
      </>
    )
  }

  {
    view === 'unknown' && errorMessage && (
      <p
        role="alert"
        tabindex="-1"
        class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-red-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-red-700"
      >
        {errorMessage}
      </p>
    )
  }

  {/* ===================================== the card actions' receipts ===

      NO `view ===` GUARD ON THESE FOUR, and that is the point. They sat behind
      one while the only buttons that produced them were on this one screen —
      and became silently wrong the moment one was not. A man who presses "Text
      Marco" in the Overdue list and is told nothing has been shown a dead
      button, whatever the server actually did. They render above the lists on
      whichever view he pressed from; `here()` is what sends him back to it.
  */}
  {
    bulkResult && (
      <Flash tone="ok" class="mt-4">
        {bulkResult}
      </Flash>
    )
  }

  {
    reminded && (
      <Flash tone="ok" class="mt-4">
        Saved to your reminders for next week. It is on <a href="/app/reminders" class="link">your
        own list</a>, not the law's.
      </Flash>
    )
  }

  {
    /* The way back, in the receipt itself. Taking a man off the board with no
       visible undo is how somebody loses a driver inside the app. */
    retiredId && (
      <Flash tone="note" class="mt-4">
        {retiredName} is off your list. Their records stay on file.{' '}
        <a href={`/app/drivers/${retiredId}`} class="link">
          Open their page
        </a>{' '}
        to put them back to Active.
      </Flash>
    )
  }

  {
    askedLine && (
      <Flash tone={askedOutcome === 'sms' || askedOutcome === 'email' ? 'ok' : 'note'} class="mt-4">
        {askedLine}
      </Flash>
    )
  }

  {
    view === 'unknown' && withdrawn && (
      <Flash tone="note" class="mt-4">
        Date withdrawn. We keep it in the history, so you can still see what was on file and
        when. The next most recent date is now the one we use.
      </Flash>
    )
  }

  {/* The receipt is a SIBLING of the editor form below, never nested — a form
      inside a form is invalid HTML and the inner one is silently dropped. */}
  {
    view === 'unknown' && savedCount !== null && (
      <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50/60 p-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-emerald-800">
          {savedHeadline(savedCount, clearedCount ?? savedCount)}
        </p>
        {overdueRose && (
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-emerald-900">
            One of these dates is already past, so it moved to{' '}
            <strong class="font-medium">Overdue</strong> instead of leaving this list. It was
            already late. We could not see it until you gave us the date.
          </p>
        )}
        {receipt.length > 0 && (
          <>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-emerald-900">
              Check these dates now. A wrong year is easy to miss later.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-3 divide-y divide-emerald-200/70 border-t border-emerald-200/70">
              {receipt.map((r) => (
                <li class="flex items-center gap-3 py-2">
                  <span class="min-w-0 flex-1">
                    <span class="block truncate text-sm">{r.label}</span>
                    <span class="block text-xs tabular-nums text-emerald-900">{r.on}</span>
                  </span>
                  <form method="POST" class="shrink-0">
                    <input type="hidden" name="record_id" value={r.id} />
                    <button
                      type="submit"
                      name="intent"
                      value="withdraw_date"
                      class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm"
                    >
                      Withdraw
                    </button>
                  </form>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </>
        )}
      </div>
    )
  }

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- list state */}
  {
    view === 'unknown' && !focus && editor.subjectCount > 0 && (
      <section class="mt-6">
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
          We need a date from you{' '}
          <span class="ml-2 text-sm font-normal text-ink-700 tabular-nums">{unknown.length}</span>
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-ink-700">
          These are not in the "need attention" number, because we cannot put them on a clock.
          They are not OK either. Without the date, your risk is the same as if it had already
          expired. That is why they sit with the overdue ones. Open one record, type what you
          know, and leave the rest for later.
        </p>

        {editor.groups.map((g) => (
          <>
            <h3 class:list={['mt-5', cardHeading]}>{g.title}</h3>

            {/* ONE PRESS FOR THE WHOLE ROSTER. On a fleet of twelve, pressing
                the same button twelve times is the work this product exists to
                remove.
                
                It used to need TWO askable drivers, on the reasoning that with
                one it is the card's own button wearing a different coat. That
                reasoning was wrong about the screen it lands on: on a real
                roster most drivers have nothing askable, so the bar never
                appeared at all, and the fastest way to ask the one driver who
                can be asked was to find his card among the ones that cannot.
                One is enough, and the words change with the number. */}
            {g.key === 'driver' && bulkTargets.length > 0 && (
              <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-brand-600/25 bg-champagne-50 p-4">
                <p class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-sm">
                  {bulkTargets.length === 1 ? (
                    <>
                      One of them can send their own dates in. The rest are things you record
                      yourself.
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <>
                      Ask all {bulkTargets.length} of them at once. Each one gets a link for their
                      own missing dates — not a shared list.
                    </>
                  )}
                </p>
                <button
                  type="submit"
                  form={ACTION_FORMS.askAll}
                  class="btn btn-primary btn-sm shrink-0"
                >
                  {bulkTargets.length === 1 ? 'Ask them' : `Ask all ${bulkTargets.length}`}
                </button>
              </div>
            )}

            <div class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              {/* NOT one <a> round the whole card any more. The card carries its
                  own actions now, a button inside a link is invalid HTML, and
                  <summary> has to be the first child of <details> to be the
                  control — so the name, the count and the menu are one card and
                  "Type the dates in" is the first thing in it. The whole of
                  that reasoning is at the top of DateSubjectActions.astro. */}
              {g.subjects.map((subject) => (
                <DateSubjectActions
                  subject={subject}
                  reach={driverReach.get(subject.id)?.reach}
                  firstName={firstNameOf(subject.id)}
                  recordHref={subjectHref(subject.type, subject.id)}
                  forms={ACTION_FORMS}
                  chase={chaseByDriver.get(subject.id)}
                />
              ))}
            </div>
          </>
        ))}

        {editor.unrecordable > 0 && (
          /* Said out loud rather than quietly dropped. A row he can see on the
             dashboard and cannot find here is a list he stops believing. */
          <p class="mt-5 text-xs text-ink-700 tabular-nums">
            {editor.unrecordable} more are about people or places we cannot save a date for yet,
            so they are not on this screen.
          </p>
        )}
      </section>
    )
  }

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------- focused state */}
  {
    view === 'unknown' && focus && (
      <form method="POST" class="mt-6">
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
          {focus.label}{' '}
          <span class="ml-2 text-sm font-normal text-ink-700 tabular-nums">
            {focus.noun} · {focus.questionCount} missing
          </span>
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-ink-700">
          Type what you have in front of you. Leave the rest for later. A blank box changes
          nothing.
        </p>

        {/* The same actions as the card he came from — under the heading rather
            than beside it, so the panel has the width to be read on a phone and
            pushes the form down instead of covering the first date box. The
            "Type the dates in" line is dropped: it would link to this page. */}
        <DateSubjectActions
          subject={focus}
          reach={driverReach.get(focus.id)?.reach}
          firstName={firstNameOf(focus.id)}
          recordHref={subjectHref(focus.type, focus.id)}
          forms={ACTION_FORMS}
          chase={chaseByDriver.get(focus.id)}
          variant="inline"
        />

        <div class="mt-3 rounded-xl border border-ink-100 bg-white">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 border-b border-ink-100 px-5 py-3">
            <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-xs text-ink-700 tabular-nums">
              {focus.rowCount} {focus.rowCount === 1 ? 'row' : 'rows'} on the dashboard, from{' '}
              {focus.questionCount} {focus.questionCount === 1 ? 'date' : 'dates'}
            </span>
            <a
              href="/app?show=unknown"
              class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm shrink-0"
            >
              Back to all {editor.subjectCount}
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-4 p-5">
            {focus.asked.map((q) => (
              <div>
              {q.writable ? (
                <label class="block">
                  <span class="block text-sm font-medium">{q.label}</span>
                  <input
                    type="date"
                    name={q.field}
                    min="1900-01-01"
                    max={maxFor(q, todayIso)}
                    class={dateField}
                  />
                </label>
              ) : (
                /* Never an input.
                   `hire_date` and `cdl_expires` live on the driver's own row, and
                   deadlines.ts projects them in with `if (a[anchorKey]) continue` —
                   so a compliance record SILENTLY OUTRANKS the column. A typo typed
                   here would override a correct hire date forever, move five federal
                   rules onto wrong dates, and leave his own page still showing the
                   right one. `dot_number` is not a date at all. */
                <div class="rounded-lg border border-brand-500/25 bg-champagne-50 px-4 py-3">
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium">{q.label}</p>
                  <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-ink-700">
                    This date lives on {q.elsewhere?.where ?? 'another screen'}, not here. We keep
                    one answer for it, not two.
                  </p>
                  {q.elsewhere?.href && (
                    <a
                      href={q.elsewhere.href}
                      class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm mt-2"
                    >
                      Open it →
                    </a>
                  )}
                </div>
              )}

              {q.writable && (
                <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs text-ink-700">
                  <strong class="font-medium text-ink-700">
                    {q.kind === 'last_done' ? 'Last done.' : 'Expires.'}
                  </strong>{' '}
                  {q.help}
                </p>
              )}

              {/* Which rows this one date takes off the dashboard. Never hidden:
                  a row he saw on the list and cannot find here is a list he
                  stops believing. */}
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-700">
                {q.settles.length > 1 && `This date answers ${q.settles.length} rules: `}
                {q.settles.map((r, n) => (
                  <>
                    {n > 0 && ' · '}
                    <a
                      href={r.sourceUrl}
                      target="_blank"
                      rel="noopener noreferrer"
                      class="link inline-flex min-h-11 items-center"
                    >
                      {r.title === q.label ? r.citation : r.title}
                    </a>
                  </>
                ))}
              </p>
              </div>
            ))}

            {focus.onlyIfItApplies.length > 0 && (
              /* Behind a disclosure because these are blank for almost every
                 carrier, and listing the Skill Performance Evaluation expiry as
                 an outstanding item for every driver — when well under 1% hold
                 one — teaches him to ignore the whole list.

                 A closed <details> still submits what is inside it. That is the
                 behaviour we want after a save, but it is not what anyone
                 expects, so the summary says the count and never hides a filled
                 box silently. */
              <details class="border-t border-ink-100 pt-4">
                <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center text-sm font-medium text-ink-700 underline underline-offset-2 select-none">
                  {focus.onlyIfItApplies.length} more that only apply in some cases
                </summary>
                <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
                  {focus.onlyIfItApplies.map((q) => (
                    <div>
              {q.writable ? (
                <label class="block">
                  <span class="block text-sm font-medium">{q.label}</span>
                  <input
                    type="date"
                    name={q.field}
                    min="1900-01-01"
                    max={maxFor(q, todayIso)}
                    class={dateField}
                  />
                </label>
              ) : (
                /* Never an input.
                   `hire_date` and `cdl_expires` live on the driver's own row, and
                   deadlines.ts projects them in with `if (a[anchorKey]) continue` —
                   so a compliance record SILENTLY OUTRANKS the column. A typo typed
                   here would override a correct hire date forever, move five federal
                   rules onto wrong dates, and leave his own page still showing the
                   right one. `dot_number` is not a date at all. */
                <div class="rounded-lg border border-brand-500/25 bg-champagne-50 px-4 py-3">
                  <p class="text-sm font-medium">{q.label}</p>
                  <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-ink-700">
                    This date lives on {q.elsewhere?.where ?? 'another screen'}, not here. We keep
                    one answer for it, not two.
                  </p>
                  {q.elsewhere?.href && (
                    <a
                      href={q.elsewhere.href}
                      class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm mt-2"
                    >
                      Open it →
                    </a>
                  )}
                </div>
              )}

              {q.writable && (
                <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs text-ink-700">
                  <strong class="font-medium text-ink-700">
                    {q.kind === 'last_done' ? 'Last done.' : 'Expires.'}
                  </strong>{' '}
                  {q.help}
                </p>
              )}

              {/* Which rows this one date takes off the dashboard. Never hidden:
                  a row he saw on the list and cannot find here is a list he
                  stops believing. */}
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-700">
                {q.settles.length > 1 && `This date answers ${q.settles.length} rules: `}
                {q.settles.map((r, n) => (
                  <>
                    {n > 0 && ' · '}
                    <a
                      href={r.sourceUrl}
                      target="_blank"
                      rel="noopener noreferrer"
                      class="link inline-flex min-h-11 items-center"
                    >
                      {r.title === q.label ? r.citation : r.title}
                    </a>
                  </>
                ))}
              </p>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </details>
            )}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink-100 bg-white p-4">
          <span class="flex-1 text-sm text-ink-500">
            A blank box changes nothing. Saving adds a new entry. Nothing already saved is
            erased.
          </span>
          <button
            type="submit"
            name="intent"
            value="save_unknown_dates"
            class="btn btn-primary"
          >
            Save these dates
          </button>
        </div>
      </form>
    )
  }

  {/*
    ONE FORM FOR EVERY BOX ON THE PAGE.

    HTML forbids a form inside a form and forbids a form wrapping part of a
    table, and this page has up to three tables in three sections. The `form=`
    attribute is what lets a box standing in a table cell — and a Save button
    standing under it — submit into this one, which is empty apart from the
    intent. The same trick the truck page uses for its Withdraw buttons.

    No script, and no form per row: on a page with no JavaScript a form per row
    is one whole page load per date. This way he types the three he has in front
    of him and presses Save once.
  */}
  <form method="POST" id="row-dates">
    <input type="hidden" name="intent" value="save_row_dates" />
  </form>

  {
    view !== 'unknown' &&
    lists.map(
      (s) =>
        s.shown.total > 0 && (
          /* `id` is not decoration. Every save on this page is a POST and a 303,
             so the browser lands on a fresh document at the top — and the man
             who typed a date into the ninth row of Overdue is now looking at the
             tiles, with no idea whether anything happened. The redirect carries
             `#section-<key>` and the browser puts him back where he was working.
             `scroll-padding-top` in global.css already keeps the sticky header
             off the heading. */
          <section id={`section-${s.key}`} class="mt-6">
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">
              {s.title}{' '}
              {/* The WHOLE section, always — never the number of rows drawn
                  below. Ten rows under a heading that says 119 is a page
                  showing you the top of a list; ten rows under a heading that
                  says 10 is a page that has lost 109 of your deadlines. */}
              <span class="ml-2 text-sm font-normal text-ink-700 tabular-nums">
                {s.shown.total}
              </span>
            </h2>

            {s.key === 'unknown' && (
              /*
                FIVE SENTENCES CUT TO TWO, and the two that survived are the two
                that change what he does. The others explained our bookkeeping —
                why these are outside the "need attention" number, why they are
                not in "Due in N days", why they sit where they sit. True, and
                answers to questions he was not asking on the way to a date box.

                WHAT COULD NOT BE CUT is the second sentence. A row with no date
                is not a row that is fine, and every version of this page has had
                to say so in words: it is the one place this product could be
                read as telling an owner he is safe when nobody knows.
              */
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink-700">
                No date means we cannot tell you if these are OK. Until you type one, treat them
                as if they had already expired.{' '}
                {/* The bridge. This table is for reading; the editor behind this
                    link is for answering, and without a way across, the reading
                    view is the only one he ever finds. */}
                <a href="/app?show=unknown" class="link inline-flex min-h-11 items-center font-medium">
                  Fill these in →
                </a>
              </p>
            )}

            {s.shown.reminders.length > 0 && (
              /* Said above the table, every time reminders appear in one.
                 Colour alone is not allowed to carry this: the badge in the
                 last column says the word, and this line says what the word
                 means. Short, because it is read on a phone.

                 Keyed off the SHOWN reminders, not off the section's. A cap
                 that trimmed every reminder out of the table would otherwise
                 leave a line explaining a badge that is not on screen. */
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink-700">
                {REMINDER_ORIGIN_NOTE}{' '}
                <a href="/app/reminders" class="link inline-flex min-h-11 items-center font-medium">
                  My reminders →
                </a>
              </p>
            )}

            <div class={`mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border ${s.tone}`}>
              <table class="w-full text-sm sm:min-w-[48rem]">
                {/*
                  No header line on a phone. Four column names above blocks that
                  are not columns explain nothing — each block says what it is in
                  its own words instead.
                */}
                <thead class="hidden border-b border-ink-100 text-left text-xs tracking-wide text-ink-700 uppercase sm:table-header-group">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">What</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Who or what</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Due</th>
                    {/* Last, because it is the thing you DO about the row
                        rather than another fact about it. The citation used to
                        have a column here; it now sits under the rule's name,
                        where the thing it cites is. */}
                    <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Record it</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-ink-100">
                  {s.shown.items.map((i) => {
                    const pill = s.key === 'soon' ? SOON_PILL : standingPill(i.status.standing)
                    return (
                      /* The block leads with WHAT is due. The section heading
                         above it has already said which bucket every row in it
                         is in, so the pill is not the thing the phone reader is
                         scanning this list for — the name of the thing is. */
                      <tr class="block py-3 hover:bg-ink-50/60 sm:table-row sm:py-0">
                        <td class={cell}>
                          <div class="font-medium">{i.rule.title}</div>
                          {i.status.standing === 'unknown' && i.status.needs && (
                            /* The registry's label for the date, never the raw
                               key: "Date of the DOT physical", not "medical exam
                               date", and never "mcp issued". The fallback is for
                               the one need that is already words. */
                            <div class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-ink-700">
                              We need:{' '}
                              {i.status.needs
                                .map((k) => resolveAnchor(k)?.label ?? k.replaceAll('_', ' '))
                                .join(', ')}
                            </div>
                          )}
                          {/* The citation, under the name of the thing it is
                              the rule for. It had a column of its own, which
                              put the regulation between the due date and the
                              box he came to type in. */}
                          <a
                            href={i.rule.sourceUrl}
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="link-cite"
                          >
                            {i.rule.citation}
                          </a>
                          {/*
                            THE FIGURE ON THE ROW IT BELONGS TO.

                            The total at the top is only trustworthy if it can
                            be taken apart, and this is where it comes apart:
                            one row, one amount, its own citation above it and
                            its own effective date in the sentence. A number an
                            owner cannot trace to a regulation is a number he
                            has to take on faith, and the whole argument for
                            showing money here is that he does not have to.

                            Only on rows that are actually at risk. A price
                            beside a date he still has time to meet turns a
                            reminder into a threat — and `carrierPenalty` takes
                            the carrier's side only, so a driver's own penalty
                            never appears on the owner's screen as his.
                          */}
                          {(() => {
                            const atRisk =
                              i.status.standing === 'overdue' || i.status.standing === 'unknown'
                            const mine = atRisk ? carrierCeiling(i.rule.penalty) : null
                            if (!mine) return null
                            return (
                              <div class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-ink-700">
                                Most they can fine you: {penaltyAmount(mine)}
                              </div>
                            )
                          })()}
                        </td>
                        <td class={`${cell} mt-1 sm:mt-0`}>
                          {i.subjectId ? (
                            <a
                              href={subjectHref(i.subjectType, i.subjectId)}
                              class="link-row"
                            >
                              {i.subjectLabel}
                            </a>
                          ) : (
                            <span>{i.subjectLabel}</span>
                          )}
                          <span class="text-xs text-ink-700 sm:block">
                            <span class="sm:hidden"> · </span>
                            {subjectWord(i.subjectType)}
                          </span>
                        </td>
                        <td class={`${cell} mt-1.5 sm:mt-0 sm:whitespace-nowrap`}>
                          {/* The shared badge and nothing on top of it. The
                              utilities that sat beside it outranked `.badge`
                              and drew this pill as a rounded-lg rectangle at
                              font-medium — the one screen where "Overdue" was a
                              different shape from every other screen. */}
                          <span class={pill.className}>{pill.label}</span>
                          {/* A countdown here is a promise of slack, so only a
                              `current` row gets one. See `dueLine`. */}
                          <span class="ml-2 tabular-nums sm:mt-1 sm:ml-0 sm:block">{dueLine(i)}</span>
                        </td>
                        {/*
                          THE DATE, ON THE ROW. He is looking at the row that
                          names the licence and holding the new licence; this is
                          where the answer goes, not four screens away.

                          The label is real and visible, not a placeholder. Two
                          different questions wear this same control — "when does
                          the new one expire" and "when was it done" — and an
                          owner who reads the wrong one types a date that is
                          right for the other question and wrong for this row.

                          A row with no box is a row whose answer is not a date
                          we can file: a terminal, an employee, a record we
                          cannot name. Its name in the second column is still a
                          link to the record, which is where that answer lives.
                        */}
                        <td class={`${cell} mt-2 sm:mt-0`}>
                          {(() => {
                            const a = answerable.get(rowField(i.rule.code, i.subjectId ?? carrierId))
                            if (!a) return null
                            return (
                              <>
                                <label class="field-hint block" for={a.field}>
                                  {rowPrompt(a)}
                                </label>
                                <input
                                  type="date"
                                  id={a.field}
                                  name={a.field}
                                  form="row-dates"
                                  max={rowMax(a, todayIso)}
                                  class="field-input mt-1 tabular-nums"
                                />
                              </>
                            )
                          })()}
                        </td>
                      </tr>
                    )
                  })}

                  {/* ------------------------------------ the owner's own rows

                      In the SAME table, under the same heading, ranked in the
                      same bucket — because he has one morning and one list to
                      work through, and a second table further down the page is
                      a table nobody scrolls to on a phone.

                      Told apart three ways, none of them new vocabulary and none
                      of them colour alone:

                        1. The last column. Every regulation prints a CFR or CVC
                           citation there, linked to the text of the law. These
                           print the words "Your reminder" and link to nothing,
                           because there is nothing to look up. That column is
                           where an owner already looks to find out what the rule
                           is, so it is where the answer "there is no rule"
                           belongs.
                        2. The tint, in the same info blue the page already uses
                           for "this one is yours to judge" — the `unsupported`
                           pill and the "You have to check these yourself" block.
                        3. The sentence above the table, which says what the
                           badge means in words.

                      The standing pill and the date line are NOT changed. Late
                      is late whoever set the date, and a second vocabulary for
                      time would be the thing that actually confuses him.

                      Last within the bucket, deliberately. When a federal
                      deadline and an errand are both overdue, the one that stops
                      a truck is the one to read first.
                  */}
                  {s.shown.reminders.map((r) => {
                    const pill = s.key === 'soon' ? SOON_PILL : standingPill(r.status.standing)
                    return (
                      <tr class="block bg-champagne-50 py-3 hover:bg-champagne sm:table-row sm:py-0">
                        <td class={cell}>
                          <div class="font-medium">{r.title}</div>
                          {r.note && (
                            <div class="mt-0.5 max-w-sm text-xs text-ink-700">{r.note}</div>
                          )}
                        </td>
                        <td class={`${cell} mt-1 sm:mt-0`}>
                          {r.subject.href ? (
                            <a
                              href={r.subject.href}
                              class="link-row"
                            >
                              {r.subject.label}
                            </a>
                          ) : (
                            <span>{r.subject.label}</span>
                          )}
                          {/* The words the nav uses — "Trucks", "Drivers" — not
                              the column names. */}
                          <span class="text-xs text-ink-700 sm:block">
                            <span class="sm:hidden"> · </span>
                            {r.subject.kind === 'carrier'
                              ? 'your company'
                              : r.subject.kind === 'vehicle'
                                ? 'truck'
                                : 'driver'}
                          </span>
                        </td>
                        <td class={`${cell} mt-1.5 sm:mt-0 sm:whitespace-nowrap`}>
                          <span class={pill.className}>{pill.label}</span>
                          <span class="ml-2 tabular-nums sm:mt-1 sm:ml-0 sm:block">{dueLine(r)}</span>
                        </td>
                        <td class={`${cell} mt-1.5 text-xs sm:mt-0`}>
                          {/* No link. Nothing to look up IS the fact. */}
                          {/* `.badge` for the shape, so it is the same pill as
                              the standing beside it and the same one
                              /app/reminders draws. */}
                          <span class:list={['badge', REMINDER_ORIGIN.className]}>
                            {REMINDER_ORIGIN.label}
                          </span>
                        </td>
                        {/* The same number of cells as a rule row, so the
                            desktop table does not shear. A reminder is the
                            owner's own note with a date he chose — there is no
                            compliance record behind it to file, and /app/reminders
                            is where its date is changed. */}
                        <td class={cell} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </tr>
                    )
                  })}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            {/*
              The rest of the section, one tap away.

              OUTSIDE the table, not in a last row of it. On a phone it belongs
              to the whole section rather than to a row, and from `sm` up the
              table is `min-w-[48rem]` inside `overflow-x-auto` — a link placed
              in there would sit off the right edge on a narrow screen, findable
              only by somebody who already knew it was there, which is nobody.

              The destination is `?show=` — the same URL the stat tile at the
              top of the page already points at. No second way of saying "show
              me the overdue ones", and no script: this is a link.
            */}
            {s.shown.more && (
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
                <a
                  href={s.shown.more.href}
                  class="link inline-flex min-h-11 items-center font-medium"
                >
                  {s.shown.more.label}
                </a>
              </p>
            )}

            {/*
              ONE SAVE PER SECTION, ALL INTO THE SAME FORM, AND IT FOLLOWS HIM
              DOWN THE LIST.

              `sticky bottom-0` is the truck page's bar, for the same reason and
              at a worse scale: "We need a date from you" runs to 28 rows and
              5,000px on a phone, so a button under the last of them was a
              button you reached by scrolling past everything you had not typed.
              Pinned, it sits at the foot of the screen the whole way down the
              section — and because it is sticky INSIDE the section rather than
              fixed to the window, it belongs to the list he is actually in and
              leaves with it. Two sections never argue over one bar.

              LAST CHILD, deliberately. Sticky only pins while its own parent is
              still on screen, so a bar placed above the "show the rest" link
              would let go of the bottom of the page a paragraph early.

              Several submit buttons on one form is plain HTML and they are
              identical: whichever he presses sends every box he typed, in any
              section. Drawn only where the section HAS a box — a Save under
              rows that cannot take a date is a button that does nothing, which
              is the fastest way to teach somebody the screen is broken.
            */}
            {s.shown.items.some((i) =>
              answerable.has(rowField(i.rule.code, i.subjectId ?? carrierId)),
            ) && (
              <div class="sticky bottom-0 mt-3 rounded-xl border border-ink-100 bg-white/95 p-4 backdrop-blur">
                {/*
                  THE REFUSAL TRAVELS WITH THE BAR. The banner at the top of the
                  page is no use once the redirect puts him back at this
                  section, thousands of pixels below it — a message up there is
                  a save that failed in silence. Printed in both places rather
                  than moved: a refusal with no section to anchor to still has
                  to be readable.
                */}
                {/* No `view !== 'unknown'` guard here: these sections only
                    render on the other views at all, so the compiler is right
                    that it can never be false. The guard stays on the banner at
                    the top of the page, where both views can reach it. */}
                {errorMessage && (
                  <p class="mb-3 rounded-lg bg-red-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-red-700">
                    {errorMessage}
                  </p>
                )}
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                  {/*
                    THE WORDS "ON THIS PAGE" ARE LOAD-BEARING.

                    A short section's bar sits in the flow while the next
                    section's bar is pinned below it, so for a few hundred
                    pixels of scrolling there are two bars on screen, both
                    reading Save dates — measured at the Overdue / need-a-date
                    boundary, about 600px of it. Two identical buttons invite
                    exactly one question: which one saves MY row?

                    The honest answer is either of them, because every box on
                    the page posts into one form — so the bar says so, and the
                    question stops being worth asking. Hiding one of the bars
                    was the other way to fix it, and it is the wrong one: the
                    section he cannot see a button for is the section he thinks
                    he cannot save.
                  */}
                  <span class="flex-1 text-sm text-ink-500">
                    Saves every date you typed on this page. A blank box changes nothing.
                  </span>
                  {/* The button carries its own section. Several submit buttons
                      share one form, and the one he pressed is the only thing
                      that says where on the page he was standing — which is
                      where the 303 has to put him back. */}
                  <button
                    type="submit"
                    form="row-dates"
                    name="from"
                    value={s.key}
                    class="btn btn-primary"
                  >
                    Save dates
                  </button>
                </div>
              </div>
            )}
          </section>
        ),
    )
  }

  {/*
    The two trailing blocks belong to the unfiltered page only. A filter names an
    exact subset; leaving "you have to check these yourself" sitting under a
    heading that says Overdue would put rows on screen that are not overdue,
    which is the one thing a filter must never do.
  */}
  {
    view === 'all' && manual.length > 0 && (
      <section class="mt-6">
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">You have to check these yourself</h2>
        <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-ink-700">
          These are real duties, but we cannot put a date on them. Some are ongoing duties with no
          deadline. For the rest, the schedule depends on something no public record holds. We
          list them here instead of making up a date.
        </p>
        {/*
          Blue, where the section above it is amber, and the separation is the
          point. These two sat adjacent in the same colour while meaning opposite
          things: one is a clock running down on a specific document, the other is
          a standing duty with no clock at all that will read exactly the same
          next year. Sharing amber made the permanent thing look urgent and, far
          worse, made the urgent thing look permanent.
        */}
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2">
          {manual.map((i) => (
            <li class="rounded-lg border border-blue-100 bg-blue-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm">
              <span class="font-medium">{i.rule.title}</span>
              <span class="text-ink-700"> · {i.subjectLabel}</span>
              {i.status.reason && <div class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-ink-700">{i.status.reason}</div>}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    )
  }

  {
    view === 'all' && later.length > 0 && (
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-ink-700 tabular-nums">
        {later.length} more are up to date and not due for a while.
      </p>
    )
  }

  {/* The way in, on the resting view only. A carrier with no reminders never
      finds a feature he has not been told exists, and the sentence says what
      the thing is for rather than naming it. */}
  {
    view === 'all' && (
      <p class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-sm text-ink-700">
        <span class="tabular-nums">
          {reminders.length === 0
            ? 'You can add your own reminders: insurance, truck payment, appointments.'
            : reminderLater.length > 0
              ? `${reminderLater.length} of your own reminders are further out.`
              : 'Your own reminders are in the list above.'}
        </span>
        <a href="/app/reminders" class="link inline-flex min-h-11 items-center font-medium">
          My reminders →
        </a>
      </p>
    )
  }
</App>
